      "problem": "Let $a=-75$ and $n=6.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n.$ Then, $r=$",
      "worked_solution": "Dividing $-75$ by $6$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n-13<-75÷6<-12.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=-13.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by solving $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=-75-(-13)(6)=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $-75=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-75={{(-13)}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 6+{{3}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r=3.$",
